Low rank approximation (LRA) is a fundamental primitive in data analysis and scientific computing, but existing GPU-oriented methods still face a trade off between approximation accuracy and efficiency. Randomized singular value decomposition (RSVD) maps well to modern accelerators, yet its approximation quality may degrade on ill conditioned matrices or slowly decaying spectra, whereas deterministic methods such as truncated singular value decomposition (TSVD) and column pivoted QR factorization (CPQR) are typically slower on GPUs. We present QRPro, a deterministic low rank approximation method that combines QR factorization with projection angle scores to identify and eliminate nearly dependent directions during factorization. With BLAS2 elimination and early stop, QRPro reduces redundant computation while preserving high approximation accuracy. Experiments on NVIDIA GPUs show that QRPro attains reconstruction errors comparable to CPQR, while achieving up to 48.89 × speedup over cuSOLVER RSVD on synthetic matrices and up to 5.05 × on real-world datasets.